# Basic All Commands Of Linux

1. Navigation Commands

Command	| Use	                   | Example
___________________________________________________
pwd	    | Show current directory | pwd
ls	    | List files	           | ls
ls  -l	| Detailed list	         | ls -l
ls  -a	| Show hidden files	     | ls -a
cd	    | Change directory	     | cd /var/log
cd ..	  | Go one directory back	 | cd ..
cd ~	  | Go to home directory	 | cd ~
clear	  | Clear terminal	       | clear
___________________________________________________
  
2. File & Directory Commands

Command	  | Use	                    | Example
__________________________________________________________
mkdir	    | Create directory	      | mkdir test
rmdir	    | Remove empty directory	| rmdir test
touch	    | Create file	            | touch file.txt
cp	      | Copy file	              | cp a.txt b.txt
cp -r	    | Copy directory	        | cp -r dir1 dir2
mv	      | Move/rename	            | mv old.txt new.txt
rm	      | Delete file	            | rm file.txt
rm -r	    | Delete directory	      | rm -r test
file	    | Check file type             | file test.txt
stat	    | File details	          | stat file.txt
___________________________________________________________

Be careful with rm because deleted files normally don't go to a recycle bin.

3. View File Contents
> cat file.txt
Shows complete file.

> less file.txt
View large file page by page.

> head file.txt
Shows first 10 lines.

> tail file.txt
Shows last 10 lines.

> tail -f /var/log/syslog
Continuously watches a log file.

> nl file.txt
Shows file with line numbers.

4. Editing Files
>nano file.txt
  
Common Nano shortcuts:
Ctrl + O → Save
Ctrl + X → Exit
Ctrl + W → Search

Another editor:

vim file.txt
  
5. Search Commands
find
Find a file:
> find /home -name file.txt
> Find all .log files:
> find /var/log -name "*.log"

Find directories:
> find /home -type d

grep
Search text inside a file:
> grep "error" log.txt

Case-insensitive:
> grep -i "error" log.txt

Show line numbers:
> grep -n "error" log.txt

Search recursively:
> grep -r "error" /var/log

6. Permissions

Check permissions:
> ls -l
Example:
-rwxr-xr--

Change permission:
> chmod 755 script.sh

Change owner:
> chown ubuntu file.txt

Change owner and group:
> chown ubuntu:ubuntu file.txt

7. User Commands
> whoami

Shows current user.
> id

Shows user ID and group information.
> who

Shows logged-in users.
> w

Shows logged-in users and what they are doing.
> sudo -i

Switch to root.
> su - username
Switch user.

8. Process Commands
> ps
Show current processes.

> ps aux
Show all running processes.

> top
Live process monitoring.

> htop
Interactive process monitoring, if installed.

> kill PID
Stop a process.

Example:
kill 1234

Force kill:
kill -9 1234

9. System Information
> uname -a
Kernel/system information.

> hostname
Show hostname.

> hostnamectl
Detailed hostname/system information.

> uptime
How long system has been running.

> date
Show date and time.

> free -h
Memory/RAM usage.

> df -h
Disk space.

> du -sh /folder
Size of a folder.

10. Networking Commands
> ip addr
Show IP addresses.

> ip route
Show routing table.

>ping 8.8.8.8
Check network connectivity.

> ss -tuln
Show listening ports.

> curl https://example.com
Make an HTTP request.

> wget https://example.com/file.zip
Download a file.

> hostname -I
Show IP address.

11. Package Management — Ubuntu

Update package information:
> sudo apt update

Install package:
> sudo apt install nginx

Remove package:
> sudo apt remove nginx

Upgrade packages:
> sudo apt upgrade

Search package:
> apt search nginx

12. Service Management

Very important for AWS/DevOps:
  
> sudo systemctl start nginx
Start service.

> sudo systemctl stop nginx
Stop service.

> sudo systemctl restart nginx
Restart service.

> sudo systemctl status nginx
Check service status.

> sudo systemctl enable nginx
Start automatically after boot.

> sudo systemctl disable nginx
Disable automatic startup.

13. Archive Commands

Create tar archive:
> tar -cvf backup.tar files/

View archive:
> tar -tvf backup.tar

Extract archive:
> tar -xvf backup.tar

Create gzip archive:
> tar -czvf backup.tar.gz files/

Extract:
> tar -xzvf backup.tar.gz

14. Sorting & Counting

Sort:
> sort file.txt

Unique lines:
> uniq file.txt

Sort + unique:
>sort file.txt | uniq

Count lines:
> wc -l file.txt

Count words:
> wc -w file.txt

Count characters:
> wc -c file.txt
  
15. Redirection & Pipes

Output to file:
> ls > output.txt

Append:
> ls >> output.txt

Input:
> command < file.txt

Pipe:
> ls | grep ".txt"

Example:
> ps aux | grep nginx

The | sends the output of one command to another command.

16. Environment Variables
> env

Show environment variables.
> echo $PATH

Show PATH.
> echo $HOME

Show home directory.
> Set variable:

NAME="Vishakha"
Use it:
> echo $NAME

17. History & Help
> history
Show previous commands.

>man ls
Manual/help for ls.

>ls --help
Quick help.

> which python
Find location of a command.

> which nginx

